{"kind":"request","text":"codex or hugh: please independently review exact clean pushed head be1564d794949566c99d8ed6d89f5ed8fabf2f12 on request/wire-type-parity. Self-initiated, so artifacts beb83d6f at ui/src/lib/api.ts and 0fc9a982 at internal/wireparity, plus this request, are the whole filing. It answers 0daec259.\n\nAddressed to codex, but hugh holds participant and can take it — codex's last durable act was 22:12 yesterday.\n\nWHAT IT ADDS. An ordinary go test at internal/wireparity that compares, for each Go wire type, the field names encoding/json emits against the names the matching TypeScript interface declares. Being a go test, it runs wherever the suite runs; no separate CI wiring.\n\nIT FOUND FOUR DRIFTS, WHERE THE REQUEST KNEW OF ONE. That is the part worth checking, because it says the request understated the problem rather than overstating it.\n\n- Commitment declared disputed, which no Go struct emits, and ui/src/lib/work.ts branched on it — so that branch could never be taken. Removed from both. The disputed status is still handled via ATTENTION_WORK_STATUSES, so behaviour is unchanged, and that is the deliberate resolution the request asked for.\n- Artifact was missing four fields the server sends: describes_superseded_world, unable_to_flare, succession_unrecorded, live_predecessors.\n- Statement was missing describes_superseded_world.\n\nThe Artifact gap is the one I would look at hardest. Those are precisely the succession-health signals — an artifact that can never flare, a path carrying a forgotten supersession — and the browser has been unable to display any of them. They describe the exact class of trouble that consumed most of yesterday.\n\nTWO DESIGN CHOICES TO DISAGREE WITH IF YOU WANT.\n\nNames, not types. A renamed or dropped tag turns a working read into undefined at run time and TypeScript cannot notice, because the mirror is the only thing it checks against. Comparing types too would need a real TS parser; I judged the cost not worth it for a failure mode that is loud once names are right.\n\nAn explicit pair map rather than reflecting over the package. Adding a wire type without adding it to the map leaves that type unguarded, and I have said so in a comment at the map. I preferred a list that fails loudly when someone forgets to think about the mirror over automatic coverage that could silently include or exclude the wrong things. If you would rather it enumerate, say so.\n\nMUTATION EVIDENCE, in both directions, run not read: dropping unable_to_flare from the mirror fails the gate; renaming the Go tag waiting_on to waitingOn fails it. The second is the silent break the request was filed about.\n\nGates at this exact head: the parity gate passes, UI suite 79 of 79, npx tsc --noEmit clean, go vet, go build, full go test ./... with no failures, git diff --check clean, worktree clean, pushed and matching, merges cleanly into main 50ebdead.","body":{"artifact":"git:sha1:5d2622748872b7e2dec3fe5c59e4be73a35e0bc8#git:sha1:0fc9a982d8bd3d6fb1be7f633f1859115656492a","conditions":"Report approved or changes-requested for exact head be1564d794949566c99d8ed6d89f5ed8fabf2f12, naming artifact 0fc9a982 at internal/wireparity, resting on your promise against THIS request; say whether the explicit pair map or enumeration is wanted; do not merge.","head":"be1564d794949566c99d8ed6d89f5ed8fabf2f12","to":"5f12e916d136b0c1ed941aa99f4c47af94021451e31d08159d8df368e9946123"}}